    "result_text": "The full suite passes (`BUILD SUCCESS`). \n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented idempotent owner creation on `POST /api/owners` so a repeated create with an already-seen `Idempotency-Key` returns the originally created owner with `200 OK` instead of creating a duplicate (which would otherwise 409).\n\n**New file \u2014 `rest/controller/IdempotencyKeyRegistry.java`**\nA small `@Component` with a single responsibility: remembering which owner each key first created (a `ConcurrentMap<String, Integer>`). It treats blank/absent keys as \"no key\" (`ownerIdFor` returns empty, `remember` is a no-op) and uses `putIfAbsent` so the first winner is preserved, keeping the calling controller free of null-checks.\n\n**`rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java`**\n- Injected the registry and the request-scoped `HttpServletRequest` via the constructor.\n- In `addOwner`, read the `Idempotency-Key` header first. If the registry already maps it to an existing owner, return that owner mapped to `OwnerDto` with `200 OK`. Otherwise the create proceeds unchanged, then the key is associated with the new owner's id and the response stays `201 CREATED`.\n\nThe create business logic in `ClinicServiceImpl` was left untouched \u2014 the idempotency concern is purely HTTP-level (header-driven), so it lives in the web layer beside the endpoint that owns it, and it reuses the existing `createOwner`/`findOwnerById`/mapper paths rather than duplicating any logic.\n\nNote: the store is in-memory (process-local), which matches the test's single-instance scope; if this ever needs to survive restarts or span instances, the registry is the one place to swap in a persistent backing store.",
